        "evidence": "J1 (index 587) shows a sharp downward step in all three axes; J2 (640) shows a sharp upward step in N and E but not U, and J3 (645) shows another sharp downward step in N and E, with U showing a large spike at ~675 unrelated to jumps. The auxiliary plots reveal that after subtracting a slow trajectory, J1–J2 forms a clear transient: N and E show a step down at J1 and return up at J2; U does not return, but its behavior is dominated by later noise/spike, not the J1–J2 interval. J2–J3 is not a clean return: after J2, N/E remain elevated until J3 drops again—suggesting J2 is not a return from J1 but a separate event. However, J1–J3 spans a full down-then-down pattern, inconsistent with a single transient. Critically, between J1 and J2, N and E levels stabilize at a new plateau (visible in original and residual), indicating J1 is a permanent step; J2 then appears as a second permanent step upward, and J3 a third downward step. But the residual N/E plots show that after J1, the signal settles to a new mean, then at J2 jumps up and stays up until J3, where it drops again—this suggests J2 and J3 form a transient (up-then-down) superposed on the permanent J1 step. U axis does not support J2–J3 as a transient (no matching return), but N/E do. Since the instruction requires joint consistency across axes and only pairs with visible abrupt shift-and-return patterns qualify, and J2–J3 shows such a pattern in N and E (and U has no coherent offset there), we accept P3 (J2–J3) as a transient pair. J1 remains a standalone permanent step. P1 and P2 are invalid because J1 is not reversed by J2 or J3 in all axes; the N/E plateau after J1 confirms permanence.",
